WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.140 --> 00:00:05.649
A lot of us are not thrilled with the social
distancing and the same self-isolation that

00:00:05.649 --> 00:00:06.649
we're doing.

00:00:06.649 --> 00:00:12.350
But one of the unintended side effects of
all this is that the climate is doing much

00:00:12.350 --> 00:00:18.310
better than it has in a long time because
so much activity has been curtailed and shut

00:00:18.310 --> 00:00:19.310
down.

00:00:19.310 --> 00:00:20.460
And this applies to air pollution.

00:00:20.460 --> 00:00:22.740
This applies to water quality.

00:00:22.740 --> 00:00:23.939
In some places.

00:00:23.939 --> 00:00:29.400
It's actually incredible and it confirms that
we can do a lot when it comes to the climate.

00:00:29.400 --> 00:00:35.190
If we commit to it and hopefully once this
is all over, it will serve as an instructive

00:00:35.190 --> 00:00:36.190
period.

00:00:36.190 --> 00:00:37.190
I'm worried it won't.

00:00:37.190 --> 00:00:40.519
I'm worried a lot of people won't want to
accept it as a reality.

00:00:40.519 --> 00:00:41.800
But this is very clear.

00:00:41.800 --> 00:00:42.829
Let's look at a few examples.

00:00:42.829 --> 00:00:49.850
In Venice, the water in the canals is very
clear because for decades you've had constant

00:00:49.850 --> 00:00:53.609
boat traffic moving tourists around the canals,
which has stopped.

00:00:53.609 --> 00:00:58.749
Now the water has cleared up dramatically
in countries like China, where industrial

00:00:58.749 --> 00:01:00.469
activity has slowed down.

00:01:00.469 --> 00:01:04.559
They are having the cleanest air that they've
had in a very long time.

00:01:04.559 --> 00:01:09.530
We have a picture up of nitrogen levels in
the atmosphere in January

00:01:09.530 --> 00:01:11.119
and then in February above China.

00:01:11.119 --> 00:01:16.960
It is a night and day difference is the drastic
flight cancelations have been terrible for

00:01:16.960 --> 00:01:18.930
airlines business.

00:01:18.930 --> 00:01:23.220
It's been horrible for people wanting to go
visit family or whatever else they want to

00:01:23.220 --> 00:01:24.220
do.

00:01:24.220 --> 00:01:27.510
But it has significantly reduced pollution
around the world.

00:01:27.510 --> 00:01:30.950
Over in Italy, more generally, pollution levels
are down.

00:01:30.950 --> 00:01:36.290
Also, nitrogen dioxide falling drastically
in the space above Italy.

00:01:36.290 --> 00:01:42.290
Now, of course, if we do nothing with this
information, it's a factoid.

00:01:42.290 --> 00:01:43.610
It's academic.

00:01:43.610 --> 00:01:44.680
Maybe it's interesting.

00:01:44.680 --> 00:01:48.740
As soon as the virus starts to pass, everything
goes right back to where it was.

00:01:48.740 --> 00:01:54.180
But look at how quickly things improved in
areas where so many people are defeatist and

00:01:54.180 --> 00:01:56.740
they say we can't be done.

00:01:56.740 --> 00:01:58.090
It's too big of a task.

00:01:58.090 --> 00:02:03.220
Look at how quickly as soon as a lot of this
activity was reduced, not eliminated, remember,

00:02:03.220 --> 00:02:04.299
but reduced.

00:02:04.299 --> 00:02:07.290
We see things improve dramatically.

00:02:07.290 --> 00:02:12.989
Now, it's not realistic to think once the
virus passes, we stay shut down.

00:02:12.989 --> 00:02:14.090
It would never happen.

00:02:14.090 --> 00:02:15.790
I'm not suggesting that we do that.

00:02:15.790 --> 00:02:18.980
But if anything, there are some lessons here.

00:02:18.980 --> 00:02:22.670
One is a lot of stuff can be done from home.

00:02:22.670 --> 00:02:26.140
Number to a ton of stuff that was previously,
you

00:02:26.140 --> 00:02:31.670
know, an hour meeting that might involve 10
people driving around to go to the same place

00:02:31.670 --> 00:02:36.980
can be figured out by email or at most, you
know, a teleconference call instead of everybody

00:02:36.980 --> 00:02:38.710
driving to the same place.

00:02:38.710 --> 00:02:44.550
Number three, a ton of stuff that people did
in their jobs isn't even necessary at all.

00:02:44.550 --> 00:02:46.950
It's what we would call unnecessary busywork.

00:02:46.950 --> 00:02:51.230
Never mind what can be done by email instead
of in meetings.

00:02:51.230 --> 00:02:58.040
Number four, we actually can get by with a
lot less moving around.

00:02:58.040 --> 00:03:02.569
And of course, not all jobs can be performed
remotely, but many jobs can.

00:03:02.569 --> 00:03:07.490
A lot of the driving around to go to meetings
and sit in offices is just not necessary.

00:03:07.490 --> 00:03:11.830
We've known this at the David Packman show
for a long time, but look at reducing commute

00:03:11.830 --> 00:03:12.830
times.

00:03:12.830 --> 00:03:13.830
Look at reducing pollution.

00:03:13.830 --> 00:03:19.140
Imagine if everybody that could work from
home did work from home permanently.

00:03:19.140 --> 00:03:24.310
You're talking about eliminating annoying
commutes, environmentally destructive commutes

00:03:24.310 --> 00:03:25.340
for so mental.

00:03:25.340 --> 00:03:26.430
So many people.

00:03:26.430 --> 00:03:31.620
You're improving mental health for people
who no longer have to sit in a car.

00:03:31.620 --> 00:03:36.629
Predominantly people sitting in cars, going
to work, coming back from work that time can

00:03:36.629 --> 00:03:41.060
instead be used for reading or for going for
a bike ride or for whatever else.

00:03:41.060 --> 00:03:42.170
And people have been doing this.

00:03:42.170 --> 00:03:43.170
People have been

00:03:43.170 --> 00:03:47.970
saying, I'm getting just as much done working
less from home the time I'd spend commuting.

00:03:47.970 --> 00:03:53.349
I'm actually making breakfast rather than
getting, you know, a cup sit, 600 calorie

00:03:53.349 --> 00:03:55.870
frappe, a thing or whatever.

00:03:55.870 --> 00:04:00.379
I'm riding my bike in the time I would normally
be commuting home or whatever the case may

00:04:00.379 --> 00:04:01.379
be.

00:04:01.379 --> 00:04:05.090
Now, this doesn't apply to a lot of people
in many industries.

00:04:05.090 --> 00:04:08.290
Health care workers, heroes during what's
going on.

00:04:08.290 --> 00:04:11.280
Grocery store workers, of course.

00:04:11.280 --> 00:04:13.879
But there are some major lessons here.

00:04:13.879 --> 00:04:19.850
And I actually wonder whether after this is
all said and done, it leads to a decline in

00:04:19.850 --> 00:04:24.420
populations in some of the really expensive
major cities, because one of the reason that

00:04:24.420 --> 00:04:32.000
a lot of people make what for them is a sacrifice
of paying a lot more to live in an expensive

00:04:32.000 --> 00:04:35.350
city or to commute into an expensive city.

00:04:35.350 --> 00:04:36.350
And having

00:04:36.350 --> 00:04:39.890
a really long commute is because they believe
they have to be there for their job.

00:04:39.890 --> 00:04:44.880
If both they and their employers realize you
could be anywhere to do the job, I would not

00:04:44.880 --> 00:04:50.340
be surprised if you start seeing people moving
to lower cost of living areas while keeping

00:04:50.340 --> 00:04:52.820
their jobs and working remotely.

00:04:52.820 --> 00:04:54.040
It's speculative.

00:04:54.040 --> 00:04:57.250
We're not going to know for a while how this
shakes out.

00:04:57.250 --> 00:04:59.030
We're still in the middle of a crisis, but.

00:04:59.030 --> 00:05:06.130
The amount that so many employers and employees
have learned from this is quite stunning.

00:05:06.130 --> 00:05:40.160
And I'm sure we will be studying it for a
long

00:05:40.160 --> 00:05:51.280
time

00:05:51.280 --> 00:06:11.470
to

00:06:11.470 --> 00:06:11.620
come

